00:03:58.944 --> 00:04:03.944
Separate yes or no contracts are how it works when the prices in a race do not add up to one dollar.

00:04:03.944 --> 00:04:09.464
Each name is its own contract, written to pay a fixed unit if that name wins and nothing

00:04:09.464 --> 00:04:10.524
if another name does.

00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:16.264
If only one can win and someone must, a fair set of yes prices should be worth that same

00:04:16.264 --> 00:04:16.584
unit.

00:04:17.124 --> 00:04:21.644
The screen often totals more than the unit or less because of the quote and the clearing,

00:04:22.064 --> 00:04:24.564
not because a second contest is buried in the first.

00:04:25.144 --> 00:04:29.324
Every leg has a bid where you can sell and an ask where you have to buy.

00:04:29.324 --> 00:04:34.044
The printed figure might be the last trade. The middle of the spread or the price

00:04:34.044 --> 00:04:38.684
of buyer would actually pay. Add the asks and you are adding the costly side of every

00:04:38.684 --> 00:04:44.044
book. Add the bids and you are adding the cheap side. Neither total is what a sure payout

00:04:44.044 --> 00:04:49.524
costs and neither has to match the unit those contracts pay. A last print on a quiet name

00:04:49.524 --> 00:04:55.144
can sit far from the live bid and ask so a column of last prices is not a basket you could

00:04:55.144 --> 00:05:00.164
lift at once. A fee taken from the premium or from the payout breaks a neat conversion

00:05:00.164 --> 00:05:05.224
even when the printed price is already summed to the unit. Buying every yes means paying

00:05:05.224 --> 00:05:10.444
the asks plus that fee, and you collect one unit only if the rules really crown exactly

00:05:10.444 --> 00:05:15.644
one winner. A total above the payout can be the spread and the venues cut left in the prices.

00:05:16.204 --> 00:05:20.984
A total below can mean stale books, a missing name, or sellers who have not posted size

00:05:20.984 --> 00:05:26.304
on the names that look rich. A thin book finishes the job. Little size at the inside price

00:05:26.304 --> 00:05:31.164
means one small order moves that name and leaves the others alone. The sum jumps, though,

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:36.444
the race did not change, and it can stay there until someone posts the other side. Read the sum

00:05:36.444 --> 00:05:42.164
beside the size on each leg. The sum by itself is not a verdict on the field. A combined order

00:05:42.164 --> 00:05:47.764
tries to stitch the legs back together, buying yes on one name and no on the rest in one action,

00:05:47.764 --> 00:05:52.844
or selling a full set so the venue matches a package against one pool of collateral.

00:05:53.424 --> 00:05:59.924
Negative risk in that plane sense treats no one every name but one as the same position as yes on the name that remains.

00:06:00.504 --> 00:06:03.044
So margin should not charge those bets as strangers.

00:06:03.724 --> 00:06:09.704
When that netting works, selling the expensive set or buying the cheap set is what pulls the total back toward the unit.

00:06:10.204 --> 00:06:15.444
When the venue will not net or the book is too thin for the package to fill, the gap can just sit.

00:06:15.444 --> 00:06:20.524
it. None of that says which name is cheap, it says the column is not a pie chart. If

00:06:20.524 --> 00:06:24.964
someone else is not even listed, the names that are listed should total less than the payout

00:06:24.964 --> 00:06:30.024
even in a perfect book, because the missing name still has a chance. Then a sum of one

00:06:30.024 --> 00:06:35.344
is the wrong benchmark, and the rule comes before the arithmetic. Compare leg with leg on

00:06:35.344 --> 00:06:45.424
the same side of the book, after you ask whether the outcomes exclude each other, whether

00:06:45.424 --> 00:06:50.204
The gap talks about market structure. It does not, by itself, talk about who wins.

